Note: The archaic nominative and accusative plural {{m|is||brýn}} is mostly used in fixed expressions, such as {{m|is|bera á brýn||to accuse [someone] of [something]}}. The definite nominative and accusative plural ''brýnnar'' finds slightly more use, mostly in expressions like {{m|is||hnykla brýnnar}} and {{m|is|setja í brýnnar}}. This latter form, pronounced /ˈpritnar/, has also been reanalyzed as {{m|is||brýrnar}} (and is often so spelled), and has given rise to the indefinite plural form {{m|is||brýr}}. Both ''brýrnar'' and ''brýr'' are proscribed, although they have been in use since at least the 17th century. Compare Faroese {{m|fo|brún}}, plural {{m|fo||brýr}}.

