Page 2674 dilkur: -------- begin text --------
{{wikipedia|lang=is}}
[[File:Agneau et brebis.JPG|thumb|A suckling lamb.]]
[[Image:Skaftholtsrettir 2004.JPG|thumb|A '''''dilkur''''' in a fold.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|dilkr}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*dilkaz}}, related to {{m|gem-pro|*dajjaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*dēaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*dijōną||suckle}} and {{m|gem-pro|*delō||nipple}}, ultimately derived from {{der|is|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁(y)-||to suckle, nurse}}. Originally referred to the young of any livestock ([[sheep]], [[goat]]s, [[horse]]s, [[pig]]s or [[cattle]]) that are being suckled by its mother. The sense “section of a larger sheepfold” is derived metaphorically from the sense “suckling lamb”, as the smaller enclosures cling to the larger like lambs to the ewe.<ref name="pistill">[https://web.archive.org/web/20160404161710/http://www.arnastofnun.is/page/ordpistlar_dilkur Orðapistill] — dilkur</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈtɪl̥kʏr/}}
*: {{rhymes|is|ɪl̥kʏr|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# a [[suckle|suckling]] [[lamb]], a lamb at [[springtime]]
# {{lb|is|archaic}} the young of other livestock, such as a [[foal]] or [[calf]], [[suckle]]d by its mother
# an enclosed part of a [[fold]]; one of the smaller folds surrounding the main [[sheepfold]]
#: {{ux|is|Hver bær hefur sinn '''dilk'''.|Each town has its own '''enclosure'''.}}

====Usage notes====
* In the old [[agriculture|agricultural]] society, lambs were {{m|is|færa frá|færð frá|separated from}} the mother soon after the springbearings in [[June]] and driven to the mountains while the [[ewe]]s were kept and milked at home over summer (this time was called {{m|is|fráfærur}} and separated lambs were called {{m|is|fráfærulamb|fráfærulömb}} or {{m|is|fráfærnalamb|fráfærnalömb}}). Younglings would occasionally follow their mothers throughout the summer and suckle the milk; the lamb would then be called ''dilkur'' and the ewe {{m|is|dilksuga||suckled by a ''dilkur''}}—{{m|is|folaldssuga||suckled by a foal}} would be used if the same happened to a foal. When the [[mutton|meat of sheep]] and [[lamb]] became a commodity farmers stopped separating the lambs from the ewes and let them suckle their mothers throughout the summer, making them heftier than before come fall.<ref name="pistill"/>

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|m.dat-:i/-}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|suckling lamb}} {{l|is|dilklamb}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|dilkakjöt}}
* {{l|is|draga dilk á eftir sér}}, {{l|is|draga illan dilk á eftir sér||to be going to have unpleasant consequences, to be a harbinger of no good, to bring trouble on its train}}
* {{l|is|draga í dilka||to categorize people by attributes such as race or opinions}}

====See also====
* {{l|is|folald}}
* {{l|is|fjárrétt}}
* {{l|is|fráfærur}}
* {{l|is|hluti}}
* {{l|is|hólf}}
* {{l|is|kálfur}}
* {{l|is|kvíga}}
* {{l|is|lamb}}
* {{l|is|rétt}}
* {{l|is|töfludraga}}

===References===
<references/>
* {{R:Cleasby-Vigfússon|dilkr}}
* {{R:is:Íslensk orðsifjabók|dilkur}}

