From {{inh|is|non|Guðrún}}, probably a mix of two originally distinct names, both with the latter part {{m|non|rún||rune, secret, confidante}}: {{m|non|Guðrún}}, {{m|non|Goðrún}}, from {{m|non|guð||god}} ({{der|is|gem-pro|*Gudarūnō}}) and (perhaps more common) {{m|non||*Gunnrún}}, {{m|non|Guðrún}}, from {{m|non|gunnr}}, {{m|non|guðr||battle}} ({{der|is|gem-pro|*Gunþarūnō}}), with the regular sound change {{m|non||-nnr-}} > {{m|non||-ðr-}}. The latter is evidenced by the short form {{m|is|Gunna}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈkvʏð.ruːn/}}

===Proper noun===
{{is-proper noun|@@}}

# {{given name|is|female}}

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|@-r}}
The accusative and dative singular form {{m|is|Guðrúni}} is used regionally instead of the more common {{m|is||Guðrúnu}}.

-------- end text --------
Page 5738 guðspjall: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|guðspjall}}, equivalent to {{m|non|góðr|t=good}} + {{m|non|spjall|t=tidings, events}}.

Probably a {{calque|is|ang|godspell|nocap=1|nocat=1}}, from earlier {{der|is|ang|*gōdspell}} ("good news"), used to translate (as a [[calque]]) [[ecclesiastical|Ecclesiastical]]  {{der|is|la|bona annuntiatio}}, itself a translation of Ecclesiastical Latin {{m|la|evangelium}} / {{der|is|grc|εὐαγγέλιον||evangel}}, literally "good news".

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈkvʏð.spjatl/}}

===Noun===
