Page 6312 heimalingur: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
A very common [[w:folk etymology|folk etymology]] re-interpretation of the word [[heimalningur|heimal'''n'''ingur]] ({{m|is|heim||home}} + {{m|is|ala||feed, nourish}}), here interpreted as {{m|is|heima||at home}} plus the diminutive {{m|is|-lingur}}.

===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# {{alternative spelling of|is|heimalningur|heimalningur|a home-fed lamb}}

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|m}}

====Usage notes====
[[heimalningur|Heimalningur]] is the only variant listed in dictionaries, however "heimalingur" is how the majority of people interpret this word and it is commonly seen in writing, having been in use since at least 1927.<ref>{{cite-web|titleurl=https://timarit.is/page/5236386?iabr=on#page/n1/mode/2up/search/%20%20heimalingur|year=1927|edition=11|page=2|title=Þar sprakk blaðran|work=Ísland|passage=Morgunblaðið er eins og '''heimalingur''' hjá Jónasi á Hriflu, sem sötrar einokunargutlið úr sömu fötunni og Jónas frá Hriflu og hans nánustu.}}</ref>

===References===
{{reflist|size=smaller}}
-------- end text --------
Page 6313 heimalningur: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{af|is|heima|t1=at home|ala|t2=feed, nourish|-ningur}}.

===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# a rejected or orphaned lamb that is fed at the farmer's home, a [[bummer]] lamb, a bottle lamb
#: {{syn|is|heimagangur|innlamb}}

====Usage notes====
This word has been [[w:Folk etymology|re-parsed]] by a significant number of speakers into [[heimalingur]], which is {{m|is|heima||at home}} plus the diminutive {{m|is|-lingur}}, which has an almost identical pronunciation.

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|m}}
-------- end text --------
