{{wikipedia|lang=is}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{is-IPA}}
* {{rhymes|is|aːɣa|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|is|non|saga}}, from {{der|is|gem-pro|*sagǭ}}.

Cognate with {{cog|ang|sagu}} (English {{m|en|saw}}); Old Frisian {{m|en|sege}}; Old High German {{m|goh|saga}} (German {{m|de|Sage}}); Old Danish {{m|da|saghæ}}, Old Swedish {{m|sv|sagha}}, Faroese {{m|fo|søga}}, Nynorsk {{m|no|soge}}, Jutlandic {{m|da|save||a narrative, a narration, a tale, a report}}, {{cog|sv|saga}}. Perhaps related to Lithuanian {{m|lt|pasaka}}.

Compare with {{m|is|segja||to say, to tell}} and {{m|is|sögn||a story}}.

====Noun====
{{is-noun|@@}}

# a [[story]]
#: {{ux|is|Segðu mér '''sögu'''.|Tell me a '''story'''.}}
# a [[history]]
#: {{ux|is|'''Saga''' Japans er mjög áhugaverð.|The '''history''' of Japan is very interesting.}}
# a {{l|en|saga}}

=====Declension=====
{{is-ndecl|f.genplna}}

=====Derived terms=====
