Page 13394 siður: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|siðr||a custom, a habit; conduct, morality, religion}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*siduz}}, from {{der|is|ine-pro|*swe-dh-}}. Cognate to {{cog|ang|sidu||a custom; a manner; a rite; purity}}, {{cog|goh|situ||a custom, a habit}} (whence the {{cog|de|Sitte}}), {{cog|fo|siður||a tradition, a custom}}, {{cog|sv|sed}}, {{cog|got|𐍃𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍃}}, {{cog|grc|ἦθος}}, from a {{der|is|ine-pro|-}} stem ''*swe-dh-''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈsɪːðʏr/}}
* {{rhymes|is|ɪːðʏr|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# [[custom]], [[habit]]
#: {{syn|is|venja}}
# [[religion]]
#: {{syn|is|trúarbrögð}}

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|m,ar:s[in certain expressions],ir.dat-/-:i.accpli:u[in some texts]|deriv=m,ar,ir.dat-/-:i}}

====Usage notes====
* The genitive form {{m|is|siðs}} is used in set phrases, {{m|is|siðar}} should otherwise be used.
* The ancient plural accusative form {{m|is|siðu||customs}} is sometimes used.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|vera til siðs}}, {{l|is|til siðs||to be [[customary]]}}
* {{l|is|hafa til siðs}}
* {{l|is|í fornum sið||in heathen times}}
* {{l|is|siðfesta}}
* {{l|is|sinn er siður í landi hverju||[[when in Rome do as the Romans do]]}}
