Page 14976 stígvél: -------- begin text --------
{{wikipedia|lang=is}}

===Etymology===
Attested from mid-16th century. Via {{der|is|gml|stevel}} or {{der|is|da|støvle}}, from {{der|is|it|stivale||a boot}}, from {{der|is|fro|estival}}, of ultimate {{der|is|la|-}} uncertain origin; altered by association with {{m|is|stíga||to step}}.<ref name="arnastofnun">{{cite-web|title=Archived copy|url=http://www.arnastofnun.is/page/arnastofnun_ord_pistlar_stigvel|accessdate=14 March 2011|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130718163911/http://www.arnastofnun.is/page/arnastofnun_ord_pistlar_stigvel|archivedate=18 July 2013}}</ref>
Cognate with {{cog|de|Stiefel}}.

A cognate with (or possibly derived from) the obsolete Old Icelandic {{m|is|stýfill||boot}}, from Middle Low German.

Belief that it derives from the Icelandic verb {{m|is|stíga||to step}} and {{m|is|vél||a machine}} similar to {{m|is|borvél||a drill; literally a drilling machine}}, {{m|is|eldavél||a cooking stove; literally a cooking machine}} and {{m|is|þvottavél||washing machine}} is a {{l|en|folk etymology}}.<ref name="arnastofnun"/> This may be inferred from the fact that ''stígvél'' is a neuter noun while {{m|is|vél}} and the other compounds listed are feminine nouns.<ref name="arnastofnun"/>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈstiɣ.vjɛːl/}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# a [[boot]]

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|n}}

====Derived terms====
{{der4|is|gúmmistígvél
|herstígvél
|regnstígvél
|{{l|is|stígvélahanki|t=a bootstrap}}
}}

