Page 15403 svefnpurka: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{af|is|svefn<t:sleep>|purka<t:a miserable or a sleepy person>}}. The noun {{m|is|purka}} is a rare word which has quite a few other meanings in Icelandic such as “something of small height” (compare the {{cog|da|purk||a small lad}} or the dialectal Swedish {{m|sv|purka||a short and chubby woman; sow; miser}}).<ref name="visinda">{{webofscience|7082|Hvað merkir orðið purkur hjá svefnpurkunum?|What does ''purka'' mean in ''svefnpurka''?}}</ref>

The oldest written example from the ''Orðabók Háskólans'' ("the University Dictionary") of the word {{m|is|svefnpurka}} is from a Latin-Icelandic dictionary, written and published in 1738 by [[w:en:Jón Árnason|Jón Árnason]] <sup>([[w:is:Jón Árnason (1665)|is]])</sup>. There the given translation of the Latin word {{m|la|dormitor}} (from the Latin verb {{m|la|dormiō||to sleep}}) is {{m|is|svefnpurka}} and {{m|is|sísofandi}}. It is possible that Jón had in fact coined this compound himself as a myriad of Icelandic words in that dictionary are of his own concoction.<ref name="visinda"/>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|[ˈsvɛpːʏr̥ka]}}
* {{IPA|is|[ˈsvɛpnˌpʰʏr̥ka]}} {{q|[[overenunciation]]}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|@@}}

# a [[sleepyhead]], a [[lie-a-bed]]/[[lieabed]], a [[sluggard]]

====Declension====
{{is-ndecl|f.genplna:a}}

===References===
:* {{R:is:BÍN}}
<references/>
-------- end text --------
