Page 16416 týna: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|týna}}, from the root of {{m|is|tjón|t=loss, damage}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈtʰiːna/}}
** {{rhymes|is|iːna|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{is-verb-weak|týndi|týnt}}

# to [[lose]] (objects)

====Usage notes====
* The [[imperative]] mood of the verb '''''týna''''' (''[[týndu]]'') is most often used with a negative (the word ''[[ekki#Icelandic|ekki]]'').
* It's not possible to "lose" insubstantial things like ''[[von]]'' ("[[hope]]") ''[[gleði]]'' ("[[joy]]"). For that the word ''[[missa]]'' would rather be used. (the exception to this is the phrase ''[[týna lífi|'''týna''' lífi]]'' meaning literally "to lose [one's] life" that is "to [[die]]").

====Conjugation====
{{is-conj-w2|t|ý|n}}

====Derived terms====
{{der-top}}
*: ''Ég tala ekki mikla [[franska|frönsku]], og ég er búin að '''týna niður''' því litla sem ég kunni.''
*:: I don't speak much French, and I've '''forgotten''' what little I knew.
* {{l|is|týna lífi||to [[die]]}} {{q|most often used for fatalities occurring in [[warfare]] or by accident}}
* {{l|is|týna niður|týna [einhverju] niður|t=to forget [something] which was previously known, specifically something which had been learnt}}
{{der-bottom}}

====See also====
* {{l|is|týnast}}
* {{l|is|týndur}}

===Anagrams===
* {{l|is|nýta}}
-------- end text --------
