Page 16948 vangefinn: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|is|van|gloss1=too little, short of, lacking in|gefa|alt2=gefinn|gloss2=given, [[gifted]]}}. Compare {{m|is|vel gefinn||lit=well given|intelligent}}, {{m|is|illa gefinn||lit=badly given|unintelligent}}, and English {{m|en|gifted}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈvai<!-- This is correct -->ɲˌcɛvɪnː/}}

===Adjective===
{{is-adj}}

# {{lb|is|dated}} [[mentally]] [[disabled]], [[mentally]] [[retarded]], [[mentally]] [[handicapped]]
#: {{syn|is|þroskaskertur|þroskaheftur}}
# {{lb|is|informal|offensive}} [[retard]], [[imbecile]], [[idiot]], person who has made a foolish mistake
#: {{syn|is|hálfviti|asni}}
#: {{uxi|is|Hvað er að þér, ertu eitthvað vangefinn?!|What is the matter with you, you imbecile!}}

====Usage notes====
Was used between approximately 1930 and the early 2000s as a polite term to refer to the mentally disabled. By the 2000s it had become a common insult among children and teenagers, after which it fell out of favor. It was taken over by {{m|is|þroskaheftur}}, which itself fell out of favor for {{m|is|þroskaskertur}} in the 2010s.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|vangefni||mental retardation}}

===See also===
* {{l|is|vangefið|pos=adverb}} {{q|used for emphasis}}
-------- end text --------
