Page 17930 þér: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|is|ɛːr|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|is|non|þú|þér}}.

====Pronoun====
{{head|is|pronoun}}

# {{lb|is|personal}} {{inflection of|is|þú||dat|s}}
#: {{ux|is|Hún tók þetta epli frá '''þér'''.|She took this apple from '''you'''.}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|is|non|ér}}; the initial ''þ'' comes from a {{rebracketing|is|nocap=1}} of ''ð'' in the second person plural verb ending (e.g. ''hafið ér'' → reinterpreted as ''hafið þér''). The Old Norse derives from a variant of {{der|is|gem-pro|*jūz}}, from {{der|is|ine-pro|*yū́}}.

====Pronoun====
{{head|is|pronoun form}}

# {{lb|is|archaic|formal}} {{plural of|is|þú}}
# {{lb|is|dated|formal}} [[you]] (the [[V-form]] in terms of [[T-V distinction]])
#* Colossians 3:9
#*: {{quote|is|Ljúgið ekki hver að öðrum, því '''þér''' hafið afklæðst hinum gamla manni með gjörðum hans.|Do not lie to each other, since '''you''' have taken off your old self with its practices.}}
#: {{ux|is|Hvað hugsið '''þér'''?|What do '''you''' think?}}
#: {{ux|is|Verið '''þér''' sælar, frú mín góð.|Good day to '''you''', milady.}}

=====Usage notes=====
As a formal [[w:T–V distinction|V-form pronoun]], ''þér'' can have a singular or plural referent, but is always grammatically plural, similar to Danish {{m|da|De}}, German {{m|de|Sie}}, French {{m|fr|vous}}, etc. This pronoun has fallen out of everyday use, but lingers in the raw plural sense in biblical language, and finds occasional use as a V-form.

=====Declension=====
{{is-decl-formal-ppron-table}}

{{cln|is|personal pronouns}}

